clearcote: clamp the WebGPU persona limits downward.

075-webgpu-coherence fetches the real Dawn limits and then overwrote 36 of them with the persona
table, with NO clamp. Dawn validates allocations against the REAL limits, so any persona entry above
the host's capability was an unbacked claim: a page allocating at the advertised limit gets an error
a real device of that class would not produce -- a cleaner signal than the wrong constant it
replaced, and a broken page for anything that sizes buffers off the reported limit.

This is the same defect that made an upward MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE spoof a functional hazard. Measured
there: the container reports 8192 and cannot allocate 16384 (GL_INVALID_VALUE), while genuine Chrome
on real D3D11 allocates it fine. Advertising capability the backend cannot honour is never a win.

min(persona, real) keeps every persona value that is LOWER than the host's (those stay backed) and
never raises one. Absent keys still keep the real value, unchanged.

UNGATED, unlike the rest of r16: removing an overclaim cannot create an incoherence on any host or
persona, and the bug is present regardless of what the persona claims.

Not observable from JS while requestAdapter() returns null -- there is no adapter to read limits
from -- so this rests on source review. It is a hard prerequisite for ever returning an adapter:
doing that first would expose all 36 unbacked claims to the web at once.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc
@@ -163,11 +163,25 @@
   // always-on WebGL getParameter spoof (an imported profile can override).
   {
     const auto& cc_pl = ungoogled::CurrentPersona().webgpu_limits;
-#define CC_WGPU_SET(field)                                               \
-  do {                                                                   \
-    auto cc_it = cc_pl.find(#field);                                     \
-    if (cc_it != cc_pl.end())                                            \
-      limits.field = static_cast<decltype(limits.field)>(cc_it->second); \
+// clearcote(r16): CLAMP DOWNWARD. This block used to assign the persona value directly
+// over the real one fetched above, for 36 limits. Dawn validates allocations against the
+// REAL limits, so any persona entry above the host's capability was an unbacked claim: a
+// page allocating at the advertised limit gets an error a real device of that class would
+// not produce. That is a cleaner detector signal than the wrong constant it replaced, and
+// it breaks pages that size buffers off the reported limit -- the same reason the WebGL
+// MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE upward spoof was rejected. min() keeps the persona's LOWER values
+// (which stay backed) and never advertises capability the host cannot honour.
+//
+// Absent keys still keep the real value, unchanged.
+#define CC_WGPU_SET(field)                                                   \
+  do {                                                                       \
+    auto cc_it = cc_pl.find(#field);                                          \
+    if (cc_it != cc_pl.end()) {                                               \
+      const auto cc_real = limits.field;                                      \
+      const auto cc_want =                                                    \
+          static_cast<decltype(limits.field)>(cc_it->second);                  \
+      limits.field = cc_want < cc_real ? cc_want : cc_real;                    \
+    }                                                                          \
   } while (0)
     CC_WGPU_SET(maxTextureDimension1D);
     CC_WGPU_SET(maxTextureDimension2D);
